Crypto
======

Crypto is a source-build workspace for OpenSSL, OpenSSH, strongSwan, StrongMan,
and PKI. Its command-line entry point is `crypton`.

Workspace paths are in [configure.yaml](configure.yaml), host package
requirements are in [requirements.yaml](requirements.yaml), and each project
owns its configuration in `src/<project>/configure.yaml`. Versions are Git
refs; use tags or commit hashes for repeatable builds.

Quick start
-----------

    ./crypton install
    ./crypton build
    ./crypton list
    ./crypton run openssl

`install` detects the operating system and uses its package manager. It
currently supports Debian/Ubuntu-style systems, Fedora/RHEL-style systems,
Arch, Alpine, macOS/Homebrew, and FreeBSD. Package installation may require
`sudo`; macOS requires Homebrew. Use `./crypton install --dry-run` to inspect
the detected OS and package list without changing the host.

`build` accepts `all` (the default) or one project name:

    ./crypton build openssl
    ./crypton build openssh
    ./crypton build strongswan
    ./crypton build strongman
    ./crypton build pki

After all runtime projects have been built, create one target deployment
artifact:

    ./crypton export

This creates `output/crypton_installer/crypton_installer.tar`. The outer
archive contains `crypton_package.tar`, which has the filesystem files for
OpenSSL, OpenSSH, strongSwan, and StrongMan, plus the standalone
`crypton_install.sh` installer and a manifest. PKI authority output and
private keys are deliberately excluded.

Copy the single outer archive to the target, extract it, and run:

    tar -xf crypton_installer.tar
    sudo ./crypton_install.sh

The installer does not require the Crypton repository. By default it installs
below `/` and therefore updates paths such as `/usr/bin`, `/usr/lib`, and
`/etc`. Use an alternate filesystem root when assembling an image or staging
an installation:

    ./crypton_install.sh --root /mnt/target-root

Projects declare build dependencies in their own `configure.yaml`. Crypton
builds those dependencies first. The current build relationships are:

    openssh   -> openssl
    strongswan -> openssl
    pki       -> openssl

StrongMan uses strongSwan at runtime through VICI, but does not need to build
strongSwan itself. Start strongSwan before using StrongMan features that access
the VPN daemon.

If `depends_on` is omitted, blank, `None`, or `null`, the project has no build
dependency. Multiple dependencies may be separated by commas.

`clean` removes generated build/output data for conventional projects and
preserves downloaded sources. The PKI project is intentionally different: its
generated authorities and private keys are preserved and are never deleted by
`clean`:

    ./crypton clean openssl
    ./crypton clean

PKI authority output must be replaced explicitly with:

    ./crypton run pki generate --force

To remove downloaded source checkouts as well, use the explicit
`clean-sources` command:

    ./crypton clean-sources strongman
    ./crypton clean-sources

This permanently removes the selected local source directories; they can be
downloaded again by the next build. Checkouts with local changes are protected
unless `--force` is supplied:

    ./crypton clean-sources strongman --force

Source checkouts go under `sources/`, intermediate files under `build/`, and
staged target files under `output/<project>/`. Each compiled project output is
a target-root tree used by `crypton export`; it is not intended to be copied
directly over a live host. Keeping one staging tree per project prevents
files from different projects overwriting one another before packaging.

`run` needs a project name. OpenSSL performs a safe version check when no
additional arguments are supplied:

    ./crypton run openssl
    ./crypton run strongswan

For OpenSSH, the no-argument form starts the configured `sshd` server:

    ./crypton run openssh

The OpenSSH settings are in the `client` and `server` sections of
`src/openssh/configure.yaml`. KEX, MAC, and cipher lists are configured
independently for each side and should share at least one algorithm. The server section also
controls the port, bind address, password and keyboard-interactive
authentication, host-key authentication, forwarding, DNS lookup, and banner.
Use `./crypton run openssh client [args]` to run the SSH client, or
`./crypton run openssh server [sshd args]` for explicit server control. The
server runs as a daemon and keeps its PID, log, host keys, and
privilege-separation directory under `output/openssh`. Runtime changes in
`src/openssh/configure.yaml` are applied by rerunning `./crypton run openssh`; the existing
crypton OpenSSH daemon is validated, stopped, and restarted automatically.

For StrongMan, additional arguments are passed to Gunicorn.

To expose the strongMan UI on the local network, run:

    ./crypton run strongman

The server runs in the foreground. Stop it from another terminal with:

    ./crypton stop strongman

The stop command uses a PID file and verifies that the process belongs to the
crypton StrongMan installation before stopping it.

The launcher binds strongMan to all interfaces on port 1515 by default, so it
automatically follows the Raspberry Pi's current network addresses. Set
`CRYPTON_STRONGMAN_BIND=127.0.0.1:1515` to restrict it to this machine, or set
the variable to another address and port. Django's accepted host names, bind
address, and worker count are configured in `src/strongman/configure.yaml`;
`allowed_hosts` is `*` by default so the UI follows the host's current network
address. The
`CRYPTON_STRONGMAN_ALLOWED_HOSTS` environment variable can override it with a
comma-separated restricted host list for one run. The same file contains the
initial StrongMan administrator under `admin`; the account is created or
updated during the build. Change the example password before deploying the
service. `vici_socket` points StrongMan at the private VICI socket created by
the crypton StrongSwan launcher. The log endpoint uses bounded polling so an
idle log page does not block a Gunicorn worker until timeout.

For strongSwan, configure `prefix_path`, `toolchain_path`, and the plugin list
in `src/strongswan/configure.yaml`. Relative paths are resolved from the
workspace root; `toolchain_path` should contain the compiler tools. Charon and
swanctl are always built, while each plugin becomes a `--enable-<name>` option.
The list is used with `--disable-defaults`, so only the selected StrongSwan
features are built. The build uses the project's OpenSSL dependency and runs
serially to avoid the known VICI dependency-ordering issue in some Automake
versions.

PKI is a separate project. Its certificate-authority hierarchy is defined in
`src/pki/pki.json`; each authority has its own output directory, root CA,
intermediate CAs nested below its root CA, devices, and deployable bundles.
PKI depends only on OpenSSL; it does not depend on strongSwan or its runtime
tools. The project uses Crypton's OpenSSL bundle when available and otherwise
can use a system `openssl` executable.

`src/pki/configure.yaml` is only Crypton project metadata. It declares the PKI
project and its OpenSSL dependency. The certificate configuration is entirely
in `src/pki/pki.json`.

The PKI JSON hierarchy is:

    pki
    └── authorities[]
        └── root_ca
            └── intermediate_cas[]
                └── devices[]

Each authority must provide a unique `name` and `output_dir`. Each certificate
definition contains its own validity, key algorithm/size or curve, signature
digest, subject, SANs, and usage. This keeps unrelated authorities and their
credentials isolated from one another.

Validate the PKI configuration with:

    ./crypton build pki

Generate the configured authorities and device certificates with:

    ./crypton run pki generate

Generation refuses to replace an existing authority. To intentionally
regenerate the configured authority trees, use the explicit force option:

    ./crypton run pki generate --force

List configured devices with:

    ./crypton run pki list

Install one generated device into a target strongSwan `swanctl` directory.
The command copies the device private key to `private/`, the device
certificate to `x509/`, and the intermediate and root CA certificates to
`x509ca/`. The public key and full chain are also preserved under
`public/` and `chains/`, respectively, without placing non-certificate files
in directories that swanctl scans for credentials:

    sudo ./crypton run pki install \
        --authority server \
        --device vpn-server \
        --swanctl-dir /etc/swanctl

The same operation is available as the standalone `pki_installer` script and
requires only the target `swanctl` path and a generated bundle. This is useful
when the target device does not contain the Crypton workspace:

    sudo src/pki/pki_installer \
        /etc/swanctl \
        output/pki/server/bundles/vpn-server.tar.gz

Or use it through crypton:

    sudo ./crypton run pki install-bundle \
        /etc/swanctl \
        output/pki/server/bundles/vpn-server.tar.gz

Each device has both a bundle directory and a portable `.tar.gz` archive:

    output/pki/<authority>/bundles/<device>/
    output/pki/<authority>/bundles/<device>.tar.gz

The archive contains the device private/public key, device certificate,
intermediate CA, root CA, and full certificate chain. `pki_installer` accepts
either the directory or the archive. Root and intermediate CA private keys
remain in the authority output and are never installed on a device.

Running StrongSwan starts the project's `charon` daemon, not a system service:

    ./crypton run strongswan
    ./crypton run strongswan swanctl --list-sas
    ./crypton stop strongswan

The workspace launcher reads the staged
`output/strongswan/etc/strongswan/strongswan.conf`; its target runtime paths
are `/run/crypton/strongswan` and `/var/log/crypton/strongswan`. The exported
package installs strongSwan under `/usr`, its configuration under
`/etc/strongswan`, and its swanctl configuration under `/etc/swanctl`.
Because strongSwan is built against crypton's OpenSSL, `crypton export`
includes both projects in the same target package.

Charon manages kernel IPsec state, so it needs Linux network capabilities. On
a normal unprivileged account, start and stop it with:

    sudo ./crypton run strongswan
    sudo ./crypton stop strongswan

For an unprivileged launcher, capabilities can instead be assigned to the
installed charon binary (reapply them after rebuilding it):

    sudo setcap cap_net_admin,cap_net_bind_service,cap_net_raw+ep output/strongswan/libexec/ipsec/charon

Configuration
-------------

Workspace configuration is intentionally split by responsibility:

    configure.yaml
        shared source/build/output paths
    src/<project>/configure.yaml
        repository, version, dependencies, and project-specific settings
    src/<project>/compile.sh
        project build and runtime implementation

PKI also has project-specific runtime files:

    src/pki/pki.json
        certificate authority and device-certificate definitions
    src/pki/pki.py
        OpenSSL-backed generation and configuration-aware installation
    src/pki/pki_installer
        dependency-free bundle installer for target devices

To add a project, create its directory under `src/` with a `compile.sh` and a
`configure.yaml`. The launcher discovers it automatically. A project config
has this shape:

    project: example
    repo: https://github.com/owner/repository.git
    version: tag-or-commit
    build_system: internal | openssl | autotools | python
    depends_on: openssl

The project script is responsible for interpreting any additional settings.
Dependencies are built automatically before the requested project.

OpenSSL library outputs are configured in `src/openssl/configure.yaml` with the
`libraries` list.
Use `static` for static archives and `shared` for shared libraries. OpenSSL's
shared build also produces the static archives, so listing both produces both
forms under `output/openssl`. Shared OpenSSL programs use the libraries from
that isolated prefix at runtime:

    libraries:
      - static
      - shared

Useful environment variables are:

    CRYPTON_JOBS=N
        Number of parallel build jobs; defaults to the host CPU count.
    CRYPTON_CONFIG=PATH
        Alternate workspace path configuration.
    CRYPTON_REQUIREMENTS=PATH
        Alternate host-requirements configuration.
    CRYPTON_PROJECTS_DIR=PATH
        Alternate directory containing project directories.
    CRYPTON_OPENSSL_BIN=PATH
        One-run OpenSSL executable override for PKI generation.
